Because I think a lot of people who've only ever had one app and that one app was super successful, there's kind of a confirmation bias.

194
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:02,960
So, like, I'm awesome.

195
00:16:03,120 --> 00:16:06,000
I did everything right, but it's like they don't even know what they don't know.

196
00:16:06,160 --> 00:16:07,280
They don't know what they did wrong.

197
00:16:07,680 --> 00:16:10,400
They just happen to, like, hit some level of product market fit.

198
00:16:10,880 --> 00:16:15,520
So, any specific apps and lessons you learn from these failures?

199
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:16,320
Yeah.

200
00:16:16,560 --> 00:16:21,600
I mean, I think it's like failure is obviously a complicated thing because I think I learned something from all of them.

201
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:23,280
And so in some ways they were useful.

202
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:27,520
But I think from a financial perspective, it's mostly what I'm talking about when they're sort of a failure on that.

203
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:43,280
And I think the two areas that the biggest mistakes that I've learned is for one is trying to really understand and having an honest evaluation of the size of the market things that I've launched are very focused, we're very niche.

204
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:50,560
And that kind of thing, it is possible to make it work, but the economics are incredibly difficult.

205
00:16:50,800 --> 00:16:53,200
And you're dealing with a very uphill battle.

206
00:16:53,360 --> 00:17:00,320
If you're dealing with something that is only ever going to be useful to 10,000 people, then great.

207
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:13,520
That for the 10,000 people, it might be really cool, but it's very unlikely you're going to make a sustainable living on an avatar that unless your economics can be so high that each one of those people is giving you a substantial amount of money on an ongoing basis.

208
00:17:13,600 --> 00:17:16,480
And I think some of my failures were things where I was like, ooh, this is really cool.

209
00:17:16,560 --> 00:17:19,200
And it's an app that does this, you know, something very specific.

210
00:17:19,760 --> 00:17:21,680
And it doesn't really end up working out.

211
00:17:21,840 --> 00:17:33,360
And I think the other thing that I found too is just having that sense of that apps, understanding what are the ongoing costs related to an app going to be and making sure that the economics of that can balance out.

212
00:17:33,360 --> 00:17:36,960
And so in your example of my weather app, ultimately, like the app was successful.

213
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:43,360
It had a reasonably good user base, but this was in, it existed in a time before subscriptions were a thing.

214
00:17:43,600 --> 00:17:45,440
Like they just didn't exist in the app store.

215
00:17:45,680 --> 00:17:53,760
And so the economics of trying to make it so that people could continuously, you know, pay for the weather data that I had to buy for it just wasn't there.

216
00:17:53,920 --> 00:18:02,240
And at a certain point it became it's like a change from being a business to a charity because I was spending more money on the back end than I was, you know, getting people on an ongoing basis.

217
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:35,360
And that was something that I don't think I really it's easy when I'm building something to just ignore that because the costs, especially early on, are so low when you look at these things and especially with most with most if you're some kind of data service or some kind of hosting provider or something, you often will have a free tier or something that like the and if in some ways success can be your own failing because you haven't taken into account that, oh, if this, you know, if I get any amount of volume, then suddenly I'm going to be spending thousands of dollars a month supporting this app.

218
00:18:35,680 --> 00:18:40,640
And if the economics aren't balanced for that, then it can, you have to essentially shut it down and deal with that.

219
00:18:40,960 --> 00:18:42,720
And I think those are two things for that.

220
00:18:43,200 --> 00:18:52,560
It's usually when an app has failed, it's because either I didn't fully understand what the ongoing costs were going to be or I didn't sort of, I wasn't realistic about how big of a market it is.

221
00:18:53,120 --> 00:18:53,200
Yeah.

222
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:54,080
Yeah.

223
00:18:54,240 --> 00:18:59,920
The unit economics are tricky because at the beginning, it's hard to get good data because everything's so small.

224
00:19:00,160 --> 00:19:01,440
It's like, oh, I can't really tell.

225
00:19:01,520 --> 00:19:04,800
I don't really know what my CAC is or what my cost of service COGs are.

226
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:05,840
So you're just like whatever.

227
00:19:05,840 --> 00:19:07,920
And then when it matters, it's too late.

228
00:19:08,640 --> 00:19:08,800
Right.

229
00:19:09,200 --> 00:19:09,760
In some cases.

230
00:19:10,560 --> 00:19:14,000
And I will say too, the users used several terms that I have no idea what they mean.

231
00:19:14,480 --> 00:19:20,640
And I think this is another failing on my part that, like, my CAC and my customer service cost, like, I don't know.

232
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:22,720
Like, this is fun, which is fun.

233
00:19:22,720 --> 00:19:24,560
I think, but that's an educational moment.

234
00:19:24,960 --> 00:19:27,760
Cost of user acquisition and what's COGS, cost of goods sold.

235
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:28,240
Sorry.

236
00:19:28,400 --> 00:19:28,480
Yeah.

237
00:19:29,120 --> 00:19:32,240
Those were like, those were things I didn't learn until I had a SaaS company though, to be honest.

238
00:19:32,320 --> 00:19:32,480
Right.

239
00:19:32,560 --> 00:19:33,680
Like, it's, it's interesting.

240
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:39,920
Like, yeah, the different, which, which, which, I mean, just highlights kind of the world we're in now, right?

241
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:43,440
Which is where most app developers are running a SaaS business, right?

242
00:19:44,960 --> 00:19:46,160
Which you were with the weather app.

243
00:19:46,400 --> 00:19:48,000
You just didn't kind of think about it in those terms.

244
00:19:48,160 --> 00:19:50,640
It was like an app with an API, but really it was a SaaS business.

245
00:19:52,160 --> 00:19:55,760
And that's why we're here at subclub.com to educate people.

246
00:19:55,920 --> 00:19:57,840
Actually, it's not dot com.co or whatever.

247
00:19:58,240 --> 00:20:13,680
But I think there is definitely that teachable moment in that thing so far is it's just it's that's another aspect of that failing is I think it's so easy coming at it from an engineering background that I can get too excited about the engineering aspects of what I'm doing that I think that, oh, there's this cool new API.

248
00:20:13,840 --> 00:20:14,720
There's this fun new feature.

249
00:20:14,880 --> 00:20:16,240
There's this cool problem I'm solving.

250
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:20,960
And I can go down, you know, spend a month of my time building this app.

251
00:20:21,600 --> 00:20:27,840
And then, in the end, I haven't really thought about the marketing side or the economic realities or all of those things.

252
00:20:28,160 --> 00:20:33,760
And in some ways, it's like, that's fine because part of what I'm, like, what I'm good at is the engineering.

253
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:39,440
And if anything, I've been able to just engineer my way out of this problem by keeping, I can just keep building.

254
00:20:39,600 --> 00:20:43,200
And eventually, I've had enough things that just kind of naturally hit.

255
00:20:44,080 --> 00:20:47,200
And it isn't necessarily the most efficient way to do it or a way I would recommend it.

256
00:20:47,440 --> 00:20:56,720
But I think that is an aspect of my failure where it is, you know, and it's but it's also the reality of being an independent, an independent developer where, like, I don't have a staff.

257
00:20:56,880 --> 00:20:59,600
I don't have anyone else in that regard.

258
00:20:59,920 --> 00:21:13,280
And so it's not like I have a business, you know, a business, a business team or someone doing user acquisition or any of those things, which on the one hand is great because it means my costs are really low, that, you know, I, my revenue is divided by one and I get to keep, you know, and I keep it.

259
00:21:13,440 --> 00:21:20,960
So if I were a team of five people and I'm dividing my revenue by five, it's quite a hard thing to, you know, have five times the five x the revenue.

260
00:21:21,120 --> 00:21:27,840
And so it's a, it's like a trade-off that you, in some ways, it'd be great if I had both, could have both, but I'm not sure if it's actually reasonable or practical to.

261
00:21:28,240 --> 00:21:34,480
I mean, really though, that's, it's, it's a good algorithm for finding, you know, new, new age.

262
00:21:34,480 --> 00:21:37,280
New APIs are the absurd version of the market shifting, right?

263
00:21:37,520 --> 00:21:38,800
It's when something gets created, right?

264
00:21:39,040 --> 00:21:39,840
There's a new opportunity.

265
00:21:40,080 --> 00:21:51,120
So exploring those and understanding those and finding out how you can remix those with existing ideas that might that, you know, as a team of one where one is an engineer, that's kind of your strategic advantage, right?

266
00:21:51,280 --> 00:21:55,200
It's might it might not be ASO, it might not be acquisition, all these other things.

267
00:21:55,760 --> 00:21:58,400
It might be like, hey, what can I do cool stuff with computers?

268
00:21:58,800 --> 00:22:04,480
And I think historically that's been a pretty good ROI for a lot of companies.

269
00:22:04,640 --> 00:22:06,960
So I wouldn't necessarily call that a weakness.

270
00:22:08,240 --> 00:22:09,600
Even though it's it's both, right?

271
00:22:09,920 --> 00:22:10,640
But yeah.

272
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:13,680
Yeah, and that specifically has been part of your strategy, right?

273
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:25,120
So like you, you know, I mean, Widgetsmith, which we'll get to in a little bit, but even WatchPlusPlus, PodometerPlusPlus or WatchSmith, sorry.

274
00:22:26,400 --> 00:22:40,240
Yeah, tell us about your thinking around using these new APIs to get attention to doing something that's never been done before as marketing, which is a great way to do it.

275
00:22:40,640 --> 00:23:27,360
Yeah, well, I think it's so this is certainly something I've done time and time again, that like PodometerPlusPlus, which is after Widgetsmith, the most successful thing I ever made was the first pedometer app in the app store and it was you know when the iPhone 5s launched Apple introduced a step counting chip into it and it was the first app that took care of it and it's like for a few weeks even it was the only one and it was one of my strategic advantages is the fact that I'm just one guy who really likes to program and is pretty good at doing things quickly and that means that I can be there on day one and I think that's beneficial in sort of two main ways that being on there early is something that often gets Apple's attention and it's ebbed and flowed in terms of whether that's important for Apple featuring you or not.

276
00:23:27,680 --> 00:23:31,440
But it's never a bad thing for Apple to feature you or to get on their radar.

277
00:23:31,760 --> 00:23:43,840
And as an independent developer, that's one of the few things that I have that I can kind of pull on that Apple gets excited about where on day one, here's this app that takes care of this new thing that they're trying to sell their new phone with or whatever.

278
00:23:45,040 --> 00:23:52,080
That speed, even like a one-person team compared to like a three- or five-person team, there's a real advantage if it's just one person, like no communication overhead, no nothing.

279
00:23:52,320 --> 00:23:53,680
Like you can just do it all in your brain.

280
00:23:53,760 --> 00:23:58,480
And like it's really hard to be, I mean, I'm saying this is watching having watched our company grow so much.

281
00:23:58,640 --> 00:24:02,160
It's like, wow, the, just like getting all these folks coordinated at the same time.

282
00:24:02,800 --> 00:24:07,120
really is a different world than when it's just you yourself, like trying to put things together quickly.

283
00:24:07,680 --> 00:24:07,760
Yeah.

284
00:24:08,160 --> 00:24:29,200
I mean, and I think that that's just such an, the other aspect of this, just so much, it's, it's so, so often I can do something faster than anyone else, not necessarily because there's something magic about me, but it's just I don't have it, there's no, it's not like it does that, oh, there's a designer who will, you know, do a bunch of specs and then that's going into a, then we'll have a sprint planning meeting and we'll break up the features.

285
00:24:29,280 --> 00:24:31,120
And it's this whole thing that, like, that's not my process.

286
00:24:31,360 --> 00:24:33,040
I just open up Xcode and start working.

287
00:24:33,680 --> 00:24:41,360
And so it's, you know, maybe it means that I'm, you know, it's like, and I end up with, as long as I have a good idea in my mind, I can just be driving towards it.

288
00:24:41,600 --> 00:24:44,240
I don't need to go through a lot of infrastructure to get that.

289
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:56,720
I don't have a road map with tasks, with, you know, sort of issues that I'm working through and burning down my, like, whatever, all those software things that you need to do if you have a big team and are valuable, but I just don't exist for me.

290
00:24:56,800 --> 00:24:58,320
And so there's that extra multiplier.

291
00:24:58,480 --> 00:25:05,280
And I think being there early is just it gets attention and it creates opportunity that there's a vacuum.

292
00:25:05,680 --> 00:25:06,640
It's a short lived thing.

293
00:25:08,560 --> 00:25:12,720
If I had launched Widgetsmith a few weeks later, I don't think it would have mattered.

294
00:25:12,880 --> 00:25:16,000
It would have completed like it is this very ephemeral thing.

295
00:25:16,000 --> 00:26:04,320
Like, thing once, you know, once a year, there's this giant opportunity for me and I've dug, sort of dove in and taken advantage of it several times and sometimes it's worked and sometimes it hasn't, you know, like my iMessage app store apps didn't go anywhere but that turned out that was a market that didn't exist but I spent my summer making sure that I was there and if they had if they had been really important and was super cool and Apple cared about it a lot then I would have been there and you never know that ahead of time, unfortunately but that's I think just something that a small team can benefit dramatically from is like taking advantage of that and being okay with two of not shipping things that are as robust and complicated as fair enough., if I was a five-person team, it could do more or have more capabilities or, you know, be localized into more, more languages or also launch on Android or whatever those things that I, that you would imagine would be beneficial.

296
00:26:04,560 --> 00:26:07,200
I don't have those, but like, it's just a trade-off for me.

297
00:26:07,520 --> 00:26:07,600
Yeah.

298
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,720
Uh, when the app store is your marketing channel primary, right?

299
00:26:10,800 --> 00:26:11,600
It makes a lot of sense.

300
00:26:11,680 --> 00:26:13,040
We did this when I was at Elevate.

301
00:26:13,280 --> 00:26:16,640
This was a constant strategy for us was what is Apple interested in?

302
00:26:17,040 --> 00:26:21,920
Even, even for us, we were a team of ten or twenty at that stage, but like, um, adding APIs.

303
00:26:22,320 --> 00:26:22,960
Oh, yeah, sure.

304
00:26:23,120 --> 00:26:23,760
It kind of makes sense.

305
00:26:23,840 --> 00:26:23,920
Okay.

306
00:26:24,080 --> 00:26:24,160
Yeah.

307
00:26:24,320 --> 00:26:29,280
We can add that like, not on our program, not really something, but like, yeah, the benefits were tangible.

308
00:26:29,360 --> 00:26:39,920
But as you kind of mentioned, it has gotten at some point, I think for a team of that size, the benefits of being in the like what's new, I forget what the they used to always have a feature like what's new in iOS whatever.

309
00:26:40,560 --> 00:26:43,680
And you would get in that and it would be a pretty good feature, but that has gone down over time.

310
00:26:43,920 --> 00:26:50,800
So now it's like, it's exclusively the to the benefit of really small developer teams, right, that they can take advantage of that.

311
00:26:51,440 --> 00:26:51,520
Yeah.

